# eprfs-peer

Born strict (RS-GOV-03): every lint suppression in this subtree carries a literal reason, and
the native style gate holds it at zero findings. The crate depends on `elohim-epr-rea` for the
roster, on iroh `=0.92` (with the workspace's vendored `iroh-quinn-proto` patch, as
elohim-storage carries) for the endpoint, and on `eprfs-core`/`eprfs-serve` for the tier it
serves on `/elohim/eprfs-tier/1.0.0` (`TierEndpoint` behind `StewardDevicesGate`; frames use the
`serde_ipld_dagcbor` codec eprfs-core encodes trees with). It takes no dependency on `epr-cli`:
the did:key verifier arrives as a `&dyn SignatureVerifier` parameter. elohim-storage takes no
dependency on this crate (R-E3).

The tier is network-facing before admission, so its work is bounded before any request is
read: a frame deadline, a per-connection stream bound, and two in-flight pools shared by every
connection, one for admitted devices and a smaller one for everyone else, chosen from the
authenticated node id at accept (`TierLimits`), so strangers can never shed the person's own
devices. Frame bodies are read as they arrive, never pre-allocated from a claimed length.

The secret key never leaves `PeerKey` except into the endpoint builder: no `Display`, `Clone`
or serialization, and `Debug` prints the node id only.

The steward channel (spec §3.3) adds `iroh-gossip =0.92` (pinned with iroh). What crosses is
pool-policy.json's `berth.channel.crosses`, default deny, applied on projection and again on
fold. The tier serves a `ChannelStore` holding projected channel trees and nothing else, with a
constant `TierId` that names no device. Heads are signed by the device key epr-cli holds,
reached through a signer closure, so this crate still holds no device key. The head table
(`<peer dir>/heads.json`) is the one place what was folded is persisted; the steward lease view reads
it.

Gossip is gated where it can be, and known to leak where it cannot. iroh-gossip's plumtree
forwards every message it receives to its other neighbours before this crate judges it, so a
forged or unadmitted head can travel one hop further than the device that refuses it; and
`GatedGossip` gates accepted connections only, so the dials iroh-gossip makes on its own (shuffle
and forward-join hints from a neighbour) are not gated. Both are acceptable here: every receiver
verifies every head against the admitted device's did:key before anything is folded, and the
gossip carries heads only (did, root, seq, ack vector), never an event body, which crosses only
over the tier ALPN to an admitted node id. The upgrade path, if either matters, is a gate on
`NeighborUp` that drops a neighbour admission does not map to a device.

Admission reads the roster through `elohim-epr-rea`, whose device-binding plane is decided by a
grounded labelling over rows (see `elohim/epr-rea/.epr-meta`): a device stands while any of its
bindings is accepted; a retired device's contests stand only while it stands, it cannot counter the
contest of itself (no self-defence), two devices that contest only each other are both retired, and
re-binding, the contester's withdrawal, or a standing member's counter-contest restores a device.
A reload closes the connections this daemon accepted from a device it no longer admits
(`ConnectionBook`); the ones iroh-gossip dialed on its own are not closable per peer, and heads on
them are dropped as unadmitted.
